To create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current page, you can follow these steps:

### 1. **Create the Extension Structure**
First, create a directory for your extension and add the following files:

```
my-extension/
├── manifest.json
├── content.js
└── background.js
```

### 2. **`manifest.json`**
This file contains metadata about your extension.

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Page Content Saver",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the contents of the current page.",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```

### 3. **`content.js`**
This script will be executed in the context of the active tab when the user clicks on the extension icon.

```javascript
chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, function(tabs) {
  const tab = tabs[0];
  const url = tab.url;
  const title = tab.title;

  // Save the page content to a file
  savePageContent(url, title);
});

function savePageContent(url, title) {
  const fileName = `page_${title.replace(/\s/g, '_')}.html`;
  const fileContent = `<html><head><title>${title}</title></head><body><h1>${title}</h1><p>${url}</p></body></html>`;

  // Create a new window to save the file
  chrome.windows.create({
    url: 'file://' + __dirname + '/save.html',
    type: 'popup',
    width: 400,
    height: 200
  });

  // Open the popup window with the file content
  chrome.windows.open('file://' + __dirname + '/save.html', '_blank');
}

// The popup.html file is used to display the saved content
```

### 4. **`background.js`**
This script handles the background functionality of the extension.

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(() => {
  console.log("Extension installed");
});
```

### 5. **`popup.html`**
This HTML file is used to display the saved content.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Saved Page</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Saved Page</h1>
  <pre id="saved-content"></pre>
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 6. **`popup.js`**
This JavaScript file reads the saved content from the file system.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const savedContentElement = document.getElementById('saved-content');

  fetch('file:///' + __dirname + '/page_Your_Title_Here.html')
    .then(response => response.text())
    .then(data => {
      savedContentElement.innerHTML = data;
    })
    .catch(error => {
      console.error('Error fetching saved content:', error);
    });
});
```

### 7. **Loading the Extension**
1. Open Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions/`.
2. Enable "Developer mode" by toggling the switch in the top right corner.
3. Click "Load unpacked" and select the directory where your extension files are located.

### 8. **Usage**
When you visit a webpage, click on the extension icon in the Chrome toolbar. A popup window will appear with the saved content of the page.

### Notes:
- This example uses a simple method to save the page content. In a real-world scenario, you might want to use a more robust method like saving the content to a database or using a service like Google Drive.
- The `manifest.json` file specifies that the extension should run in the background and have access to the active tab's content.

This is a basic implementation, and you can expand it further based on your needs.

