To get the bounding box of a deeply nested SVG object in JavaScript, you can use a recursive function that traverses the DOM tree and collects the coordinates of each element's bounding box. Here's an example:

```javascript
function getBoundingBox(element) {
    const bbox = element.getBoundingClientRect();
    return {
        top: bbox.top,
        left: bbox.left,
        width: bbox.width,
        height: bbox.height
    };
}

function getAllBoundingBoxes(element) {
    const boxes = [];
    if (element === null || element === undefined) {
        return boxes;
    }
    for (let i = 0; i < element.children.length; i++) {
        const child = element.children[i];
        const childBox = getBoundingBox(child);
        boxes.push(childBox);
        boxes = boxes.concat(getAllBoundingBoxes(child));
    }
    return boxes;
}

// Example usage:
const svgElement = document.querySelector('svg');
const allBoxes = getAllBoundingBoxes(svgElement);
console.log(allBoxes);
```

This code defines two functions: `getBoundingBox` and `getAllBoundingBoxes`. The `getBoundingBox` function calculates the bounding box of a single element, while the `getAllBoundingBoxes` function recursively calculates the bounding boxes of all elements within a given parent element.

The example usage at the end demonstrates how to use these functions to retrieve the bounding boxes of all elements within an SVG element. The resulting array of objects contains the top, left, width, and height properties for each element's bounding box.
