To achieve this effect in Jetpack Compose, you can use the `Modifier` and `Layout` functions to dynamically adjust the size and position of your children based on the available space.

Here is an example:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun DynamicContent() {
    val layoutSize = rememberLayoutSize { size ->
        // Calculate the available width and height
        val availableWidth = size.width - 100f // Subtract some padding
        val availableHeight = size.height - 50f // Subtract some padding

        // Create a modifier that adjusts the size and position of the child
        Modifier
            .fillMaxSize()
            .background(Color.Blue)
            .padding(20.dp)
            .constrainAs(child) {
                top.linkTo(parent.top)
                bottom.linkTo(parent.bottom)
                start.linkTo(parent.start)
                end.linkTo(parent.end)

                // Adjust the size of the child based on the available space
                if (availableWidth > 0 && availableHeight > 0) {
                    size.width = availableWidth
                    size.height = availableHeight
                } else {
                    size.width = 100f // Default width
                    size.height = 50f // Default height
                }
            }
    }

    Box(
        modifier = layoutSize,
        contentAlignment = Alignment.Center
    ) {
        Text("Hello, World!")
    }
}
```

In this example, we first calculate the available width and height by subtracting some padding from the parent's size. Then, we create a `Modifier` that adjusts the size and position of the child based on the available space. If there is enough space, we set the size of the child to the available width and height; otherwise, we set a default size.

Note that this is just one way to achieve this effect. You may need to modify the code to fit your specific needs.
